(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "9.1", title: "Python 3: lists, functions and files", q: "How do programmers organise larger programs and larger amounts of data?", lessons: [
{ t: "Diagnostic recap: selection and loops", o: "I can recall selection, loops and validation from Year 8.",
  k: ["selection: choosing which code runs using if, elif and else", "iteration: repeating code with a for or while loop", "validation: checking that input is sensible before it is used"],
  d: ["Write a for loop that prints the numbers 1 to 5.", "for i in range(1, 6):\n    print(i)"],
  s: ["Selection|>> if mark >= 80:|>>     print('A')|>> elif mark >= 60:|>>     print('B')|>> else:|>>     print('C')",
      "Loops|for: when the number of repeats is known|while: when it depends on a condition|Initialise before the loop, update inside, output after",
      "Validation|>> age = int(input('Age: '))|>> while age < 0 or age > 120:|>>     age = int(input('Try again: '))"],
  a: "Complete the 15-question diagnostic quiz, then fix the five broken programs. Use your score to choose the recap card you need. Support: work on the recap card for your weakest area with a partner. Stretch: write a program that asks for 10 numbers and prints how many were even.",
  w: [["How many times does `for i in range(2, 7):` repeat?", "5 times."], ["Write a condition that is True when `x` is between 1 and 10 inclusive.", "x >= 1 and x <= 10"], ["Trace: `total = 0`, `for n in range(1, 4): total = total + n`. Final total?", "6."], ["When should you use while instead of for?", "When the number of repeats depends on a condition and is not known in advance."], ["A loop to total 5 numbers prints a different total each time round. Explain the bug.", "The print line is indented inside the loop. It should be after the loop, not indented."]],
  x: ["Which Year 8 skill do you most need to practise?", "A named skill."] },
{ t: "Lists: create, index and append", o: "I can store several values in a list and access them by index.",
  k: ["list: a single variable that holds an ordered collection of values", "index: the position of an item in a list, starting from 0", "append: to add an item to the end of a list"],
  d: ["You need to store the names of 30 students. Why are 30 separate variables a bad idea?", "It is long, error-prone, and you cannot loop over them easily."],
  s: ["Creating a list|>> names = ['Ana', 'Ben', 'Chen']|Square brackets, items separated by commas|`len(names)` gives 3",
      "Indexing|The first item is at index 0: `names[0]` is 'Ana'|`names[2]` is 'Chen'; `names[-1]` is the last item|`names[3]` causes an IndexError",
      "Changing a list|>> names.append('Dev')|>> names[1] = 'Bella'|>> names.remove('Ana')"],
  a: "Predict the output of the ten list operations, then run them to check. Write a program that starts with an empty list, asks for five favourite foods, appends each and prints the list and its length. Support: complete the program with the append line missing. Stretch: print the foods in reverse order using an index that counts down.",
  w: [["What is the index of the first item in a Python list?", "0."], ["`colours = ['red', 'green', 'blue']`. What is `colours[1]`?", "'green'"], ["Write a line that adds 'pink' to the end of `colours`.", "colours.append('pink')"], ["What does `len(colours)` give after 'pink' is added?", "4."], ["Explain why `colours[3]` caused an error before 'pink' was added.", "The list had three items at indexes 0, 1 and 2, so index 3 did not exist."]],
  x: ["What does `names[-1]` give?", "The last item in the list."] },
{ t: "Lab: shopping list and class register", o: "I can build programs that store, change and display lists.",
  k: ["in: an operator that tests whether a value is in a list", "remove: to delete the first matching item from a list", "menu: a set of numbered choices for the user"],
  d: ["Write a condition that is True if 'milk' is in the list `shopping`.", "'milk' in shopping"],
  s: ["Shopping list|Add an item; remove an item; show the list|Use `if item in shopping:` before removing|Otherwise `remove` causes an error",
      "Menu loop|>> choice = ''|>> while choice != '4':|>>     choice = input('1 Add, 2 Remove, 3 Show, 4 Quit: ')|One `if` or `elif` per choice",
      "Class register|Store names in one list|Ask 'present?' for each name with a for loop|Append present students to a second list"],
  a: "Build the shopping list program with a menu of add, remove, show and quit. Then build the class register for a list of six names and print who was absent. Support: complete the menu program where the add branch is written. Stretch: stop the same item being added twice.",
  w: [["What does `'tea' in shopping` give if 'tea' is not in the list?", "False."], ["Why test `if item in shopping:` before `shopping.remove(item)`?", "remove causes an error if the item is not in the list."], ["Write a loop that prints every item in `shopping` on its own line.", "for item in shopping:\n    print(item)"], ["How does the menu loop end?", "When the user enters 4, so the condition choice != '4' becomes False."], ["Explain how to find who was absent using two lists.", "Loop through the full list of names and print each one that is not in the present list."]],
  x: ["Which list method adds an item, and which removes one?", "append adds; remove removes."] },
{ t: "Looping over lists: totals, maximum and minimum", o: "I can process every item in a list with a loop.",
  k: ["traverse: to visit every item in a list in turn", "maximum: the largest value", "running total: a variable that is added to for each item"],
  d: ["Marks are 12, 18, 7, 15. Without a calculator, find the total and the highest mark. What did you keep track of?", "Total 52, highest 18. You kept a running total and the largest so far."],
  s: ["Two ways to loop|>> for mark in marks:|>>     print(mark)|or by index:|>> for i in range(len(marks)):|>>     print(i, marks[i])",
      "Total and average|>> total = 0|>> for mark in marks:|>>     total = total + mark|>> average = total / len(marks)",
      "Highest|>> highest = marks[0]|>> for mark in marks:|>>     if mark > highest:|>>         highest = mark"],
  a: "Write a program that stores eight temperatures in a list and prints the total, average, highest, lowest and how many were above the average. Do it with your own loops first, then check with sum(), max() and min(). Support: complete the total and highest sections. Stretch: also print the position (index) of the highest temperature.",
  w: [["Write a loop that adds every number in `nums` to `total`.", "total = 0\nfor n in nums:\n    total = total + n"], ["Why is `highest` set to `marks[0]` and not to 0?", "If every mark were negative, starting at 0 would give the wrong answer."], ["`marks = [12, 18, 7, 15]`. What is the average?", "13.0"], ["Which built-in functions give the total, largest and smallest of a list?", "sum(), max(), min()."], ["Explain when you need `range(len(marks))` instead of `for mark in marks`.", "When you need the position (index) of each item as well as its value, for example to report where the highest is."]],
  x: ["Name the three steps of the totalling pattern.", "Initialise before, update inside, output after."] },
{ t: "String handling: length, slicing and case", o: "I can use string functions and slicing to process text.",
  k: ["slicing: taking part of a string using [start:end]", "method: a function that belongs to a value, written after a dot", "concatenation: joining strings with +"],
  d: ["`word = 'Python'`. What do you think `word[0]` gives?", "'P'. Strings are indexed like lists."],
  s: ["Strings are sequences|`len('Python')` is 6|`word[0]` is 'P'; `word[-1]` is 'n'|`for letter in word:` visits each character",
      "Slicing|`word[0:3]` is 'Pyt': from index 0 up to, not including, 3|`word[2:]` is 'thon'; `word[:2]` is 'Py'|The same rule as range: the end is not included",
      "Useful methods|`.upper()` and `.lower()` change case|`.isdigit()` is True if every character is a digit|`.count('a')` counts a character"],
  a: "Predict and test the twelve string expressions. Then write a program that asks for a full name and prints the initials in capitals, the number of letters and the name reversed. Support: complete the initials section. Stretch: count the vowels using a loop and `in 'aeiou'`.",
  w: [["`s = 'Computer'`. What is `len(s)`?", "8"], ["What is `s[0:4]`?", "'Comp'"], ["What is `s[-3:]`?", "'ter'"], ["Write an expression that gives `s` in capitals.", "s.upper()"], ["Explain why `s[2:5]` gives three characters and not four.", "Slicing stops before the end index, so it takes indexes 2, 3 and 4."]],
  x: ["What does `'abc123'.isdigit()` give?", "False."] },
{ t: "Lab: password strength checker", o: "I can combine string handling, loops and selection in one program.",
  k: ["strength rule: a condition a strong password must meet", "isupper: a method that is True for capital letters", "feedback: messages telling the user what to fix"],
  d: ["List four rules that make a password stronger.", "Length of 8 or more, a capital letter, a small letter, a digit (or a symbol)."],
  s: ["The rules|At least 8 characters|At least one capital, one small letter and one digit|Not the word 'password'",
      "Checking each character|>> has_digit = False|>> for ch in password:|>>     if ch.isdigit():|>>         has_digit = True|Use a flag for each rule",
      "Feedback|Score one point for each rule met|Print 'Weak', 'Medium' or 'Strong'|List every rule that was not met"],
  a: "Write the password strength checker: score each rule, print a rating and list the rules not met. Test it with six passwords chosen to test each rule. Support: complete the program with the capital and digit checks written. Stretch: loop until the user enters a strong password, then count the attempts.",
  w: [["What is a flag variable?", "A variable that records whether something has happened, usually True or False."], ["Write a condition that is True when `password` has at least 8 characters.", "len(password) >= 8"], ["What does `'A'.isupper()` give?", "True."], ["Which test password checks only the 'contains a digit' rule?", "One that meets every other rule but has no digit, such as 'Sunflowers'."], ["Explain why a flag is set to False before the loop and only ever set to True inside it.", "It starts assuming the rule is not met. Finding one matching character proves it is met, and later characters must not undo that."]],
  x: ["Which rules does the password 'sunflower' fail?", "It has no capital letter and no digit (it meets the length rule)."] },
{ t: "Procedures: naming a block of code", o: "I can define and call a procedure.",
  k: ["subroutine: a named block of code that can be run when needed", "procedure: a subroutine that carries out a task but does not return a value", "call: to run a subroutine by using its name"],
  d: ["A program prints the same 3-line banner in five places. What problems could this cause?", "It is long, and a change must be made in five places, so mistakes creep in."],
  s: ["Defining|>> def show_banner():|>>     print('=========')|>>     print(' QUIZ TIME')|>>     print('=========')|Nothing happens until it is called",
      "Calling|>> show_banner()|Run it as many times as you like|Define before you call it",
      "Why use subroutines?|Write the code once and reuse it|Each part has a clear name and job|Easier to test, fix and share in a team"],
  a: "Rewrite the repetitive program on the sheet using three procedures, then call them in the right order. Write a procedure that draws a box of stars and call it twice. Support: the first procedure is written; write the other two. Stretch: write a menu program where each choice calls its own procedure.",
  w: [["What is a subroutine?", "A named block of code that can be run when needed."], ["Which keyword starts a function or procedure definition in Python?", "def"], ["Write the line that calls a procedure named `greet`.", "greet()"], ["A procedure is defined but nothing appears when the program runs. Why?", "It has not been called."], ["Give two advantages of using subroutines.", "Any two: code is written once and reused, programs are easier to read, easier to test and fix, work can be shared in a team."]],
  x: ["What is the difference between defining and calling a subroutine?", "Defining writes the code and gives it a name. Calling runs it."] },
{ t: "Parameters and arguments", o: "I can pass values into a subroutine using parameters.",
  k: ["parameter: a variable in a subroutine definition that receives a value", "argument: the value passed to a subroutine when it is called", "reuse: using the same code for different data"],
  d: ["A procedure prints a times table for 7. How could the same procedure print the table for any number?", "Pass the number in when it is called."],
  s: ["Parameters|>> def times_table(n):|>>     for i in range(1, 13):|>>         print(n, 'x', i, '=', n * i)|`n` is a parameter",
      "Arguments|>> times_table(7)|>> times_table(12)|7 and 12 are arguments: they are copied into n",
      "More than one|>> def box(width, height):|Arguments are matched to parameters in order|`box(5, 3)`: width is 5, height is 3"],
  a: "Predict the output of the six calls, then write procedures `greet(name)`, `box(width, height)` and `countdown(start)`, calling each with different arguments. Support: complete `greet` and `countdown`. Stretch: add a third parameter to `box` for the character used to draw it.",
  w: [["What is a parameter?", "A variable in a subroutine definition that receives a value."], ["What is an argument?", "The value passed to a subroutine when it is called."], ["In `box(5, 3)`, what value does `height` receive?", "3."], ["Write a procedure `shout(word)` that prints the word in capitals.", "def shout(word):\n    print(word.upper())"], ["Explain why parameters make a subroutine more useful.", "The same code can work with different data each time it is called."]],
  x: ["In `def area(w, h):`, which are the parameters?", "w and h."] },
{ t: "Lab: refactor a program into procedures", o: "I can improve the structure of a working program without changing what it does.",
  k: ["refactor: to restructure code without changing what it does", "duplication: the same code written in more than one place", "regression test: running old tests again to check nothing has broken"],
  d: ["Why should you run your tests again after tidying up a program?", "To check that the changes have not broken anything."],
  s: ["Before you start|Run the program and record its output for three inputs|These are your regression tests|Save a copy",
      "Refactor|Find repeated blocks of code|Turn each into a subroutine with parameters|Replace the repeats with calls",
      "Check|Run the three tests again|The output must be identical|Fewer lines, same behaviour"],
  a: "Choose one of your Year 8 programs (or the supplied quiz). Record three tests, refactor it into at least three subroutines with parameters, and show the tests still give identical output. Support: refactor the supplied quiz using the marked repeated blocks. Stretch: reduce the program's length by at least a third and report the line counts.",
  w: [["What does refactor mean?", "To restructure code without changing what it does."], ["What is a regression test?", "Running earlier tests again to check nothing has broken."], ["Which repeated block did you turn into a subroutine?", "A specific block."], ["How many lines was the program before and after?", "Two numbers."], ["Explain why refactoring makes a program easier to change later.", "Each job is in one named place, so a change is made once and is less likely to break other parts."]],
  x: ["Did all three tests give the same output after refactoring?", "Yes, or which one changed and why."] },
{ t: "Functions and return values", o: "I can write a function that returns a value.",
  k: ["function: a subroutine that returns a value", "return: sends a value back to the line that called the function", "return value: the value a function gives back"],
  d: ["`len('hello')` gives 5. Where does the 5 go?", "Back to the line that used len, where it can be stored or printed."],
  s: ["Returning a value|>> def area(width, height):|>>     return width * height|>> a = area(4, 5)|a is 20",
      "print is not return|print shows a value on the screen|return gives it back to the program to use|A function with no return gives back None",
      "Using the result|>> total = area(4, 5) + area(2, 3)|>> if area(4, 5) > 10:|The call is replaced by its return value"],
  a: "Write and test five functions: `area`, `average`, `is_even`, `larger` and `count_vowels`. Check `count_vowels` on the Python check page of CS Toolkit. Support: complete `area` and `is_even`. Stretch: write `grade(mark)` that returns a letter, then use it in a loop over a list of marks.",
  w: [["What is the difference between a procedure and a function?", "A function returns a value; a procedure does not."], ["Write a function `double(n)` that returns n multiplied by 2.", "def double(n):\n    return n * 2"], ["What does `area(3, 7)` return?", "21"], ["A function prints its answer but `x = add(2, 3)` makes x None. Why?", "The function uses print instead of return, so nothing is returned."], ["Explain why returning a value is more useful than printing it.", "The calling code can store it, compare it, calculate with it or print it, as needed."]],
  x: ["What does a Python function return if it has no return statement?", "None."] },
{ t: "Scope: local and global", o: "I can explain the difference between local and global variables.",
  k: ["scope: the part of a program where a variable can be used", "local variable: a variable created inside a subroutine, which only exists there", "global variable: a variable created in the main program, outside any subroutine"],
  d: ["A variable is created inside a function. Do you think it can be printed in the main program?", "No. It only exists inside the function."],
  s: ["Local variables|>> def f():|>>     x = 5|>> f()|>> print(x)|NameError: x only existed while f was running",
      "Global variables|Created in the main program|Can be read inside a function|Changing one inside a function needs the `global` keyword",
      "Good practice|Pass values in with parameters|Pass results out with return|Avoid global variables: they make bugs hard to find"],
  a: "Predict the output or error for the eight scope examples, then run them. Rewrite the program that uses three global variables so it uses parameters and return values instead. Support: predict the first four with a partner. Stretch: explain why two functions can each have a local variable called `total` without clashing.",
  w: [["What is scope?", "The part of a program where a variable can be used."], ["What is a local variable?", "A variable created inside a subroutine, which only exists there."], ["What error occurs when the main program prints a local variable?", "NameError."], ["How should a function get values from the main program?", "Through its parameters."], ["Explain why local variables reduce bugs.", "A subroutine cannot accidentally change variables elsewhere, so each part can be tested on its own."]],
  x: ["Can two different functions each use a local variable called `count`?", "Yes. Each exists only inside its own function."] },
{ t: "Lab: build and test a maths library", o: "I can write a set of functions and test each one.",
  k: ["library: a collection of reusable functions", "test case: an input with its expected output", "import: to load functions from another file"],
  d: ["`import random` gives you `random.randint`. Where do you think that function is written?", "In another file: the random library."],
  s: ["Your library|In `mymaths.py` write: `square(n)`, `cube(n)`, `is_even(n)`, `average(numbers)`, `largest(numbers)`|No input or print inside them: parameters in, return out|Comment each one",
      "Test cases|Write at least three for each function|Include an edge case: an empty list, a negative number, zero|>> print(average([2, 4, 6]) == 4)",
      "Using it|In another file: `import mymaths`|>> print(mymaths.square(9))|Both files must be in the same folder"],
  a: "Write the five functions in `mymaths.py`, then a separate test file that imports them and prints True for every passing test. Fix any failures. Support: write and test `square`, `cube` and `is_even`. Stretch: make `average([])` return 0 instead of crashing, and test it.",
  w: [["What is a library?", "A collection of reusable functions."], ["What is a test case?", "An input together with its expected output."], ["What should `is_even(7)` return?", "False."], ["Write a test that checks `square(5)` is 25.", "print(square(5) == 25)"], ["Explain why library functions should not use input or print.", "So they can be reused anywhere: the calling program decides where data comes from and what to do with the result."]],
  x: ["Which edge case did you test for `average`?", "For example an empty list or a list with one item."] },
{ t: "Reading text files", o: "I can read data from a text file into a program.",
  k: ["text file: a file that stores characters, line by line", "open: to connect a program to a file in a particular mode", "strip: to remove spaces and the new-line character from the ends of a string"],
  d: ["When a program ends, what happens to the data in its variables?", "It is lost, because variables are held in RAM."],
  s: ["Opening a file|>> file = open('names.txt', 'r')|'r' means read|>> file.close()",
      "Reading line by line|>> for line in file:|>>     print(line.strip())|Each line ends with a hidden new-line character: strip removes it",
      "Reading into a list|>> names = []|>> for line in file:|>>     names.append(line.strip())|Numbers arrive as text: use int() to convert"],
  a: "Read `scores.txt` (one number per line) into a list, then print the total, average and highest. Then read `capitals.txt` and print each line. Support: complete the reading loop. Stretch: read a file where each line is 'name,score' and use `.split(',')` to separate them.",
  w: [["Which mode opens a file for reading?", "'r'"], ["Why is `.strip()` used on each line?", "To remove the new-line character and spaces at the ends."], ["Each line of a file is a number. What must be done before adding them?", "Convert each one to int (or float)."], ["Write the line that closes a file called `file`.", "file.close()"], ["Explain why programs store data in files and not only in variables.", "Variables are lost when the program ends. Files are kept on secondary storage."]],
  x: ["What does `'Ana,72'.split(',')` give?", "['Ana', '72']"] },
{ t: "Writing and appending to files", o: "I can write data to a text file.",
  k: ["write mode: 'w', which creates the file or replaces everything in it", "append mode: 'a', which adds to the end of the file", "new line: the character `\\n` that ends a line"],
  d: ["What do you think might happen if you open an existing file in write mode?", "Its old contents are deleted."],
  s: ["Writing|>> file = open('log.txt', 'w')|>> file.write('Hello\\n')|>> file.close()|`write` does not add a new line by itself",
      "Appending|>> file = open('log.txt', 'a')|>> file.write(name + '\\n')|Keeps what was there and adds to the end",
      "Writing numbers|write needs a string: `file.write(str(score) + '\\n')`|Close the file or the data may not be saved|Choose 'w' or 'a' carefully"],
  a: "Write a program that asks for five names and saves them to a file, one per line. Then write a guest book that appends each new name and prints the whole book. Support: complete the write loop. Stretch: save 'name,score' lines and read them back into two lists.",
  w: [["What is the difference between 'w' and 'a'?", "'w' replaces the contents of the file; 'a' adds to the end."], ["Why does each write end with `'\\n'`?", "So each item goes on its own line."], ["Write a line that writes the number in `score` to `file`.", "file.write(str(score) + '\\n')"], ["A program writes three names but the file is empty. Suggest a cause.", "The file was not closed, so the data was not saved."], ["A high-score program uses 'w' to save each new score. Explain the problem.", "Each save deletes the previous scores, so only the last one is kept."]],
  x: ["Which mode would you use for a log that grows every day?", "'a' (append)."] },
{ t: "Lab: high-score table that survives a restart", o: "I can use files so that data is kept between runs of a program.",
  k: ["persistent: kept after the program stops", "load: to read saved data into a program at the start", "save: to write data to a file so it is kept"],
  d: ["How does a game remember your high score after you switch it off?", "It saves the score to a file and loads it when it starts."],
  s: ["Plan|At the start: load scores from the file into a list|During the program: add the new score|At the end: save the list back to the file",
      "Sorting|`scores.sort(reverse=True)` puts the highest first|`scores[:5]` keeps the top five|Save only the top five",
      "If the file is missing|The first run has no file yet|Create an empty `scores.txt` before the first run|Or check with your teacher's example"],
  a: "Add a persistent high-score table to your guessing game or quiz: load, add the new score, keep the top five, save. Run it three times to show the scores survive. Support: use the load and save functions provided. Stretch: store names with the scores and print a formatted table.",
  w: [["What does persistent mean?", "Kept after the program stops."], ["What does `scores.sort(reverse=True)` do?", "Sorts the list from largest to smallest."], ["What does `scores[:5]` give?", "The first five items of the list."], ["Why load the scores at the start and save at the end?", "So old scores are included and the updated list is kept for next time."], ["Explain how you proved that the scores survive a restart.", "Run the program, close it, run it again, and the earlier scores are still listed."]],
  x: ["Which file mode did you use to save the top five, and why?", "'w', because the whole list is rewritten each time."] },
{ t: "Plan: quiz with a question file and saved scores", o: "I can plan a program that uses lists, functions and files.",
  k: ["structure chart: a diagram showing a program's subroutines and how they connect", "specification: a description of what the program must do", "success criteria: testable statements about the finished program"],
  d: ["Name four subroutines a quiz program might need.", "For example load_questions, ask_question, show_score, save_score."],
  s: ["The brief|Questions are stored in a text file, one 'question,answer' per line|The quiz asks each question and keeps a score|Scores are saved with the player's name",
      "Decompose|Draw a structure chart: main program at the top, subroutines below|For each subroutine: parameters and return value|One job per subroutine",
      "Success criteria and tests|Five 'The program must...' statements|A test for each, including a wrong answer and a capital-letter answer|Plan which file you will test with"],
  a: "Complete the planning sheet: structure chart, a table of subroutines with parameters and returns, five success criteria and a test plan. Write the question file with at least eight questions. Support: use the structure chart template. Stretch: plan a feature that chooses questions in a random order.",
  w: [["What is a structure chart?", "A diagram showing a program's subroutines and how they connect."], ["List three subroutines in your plan.", "Three named subroutines."], ["Give the parameters and return value of one subroutine.", "For example ask_question(question, answer) returns True or False."], ["Write one line of your question file.", "For example: Capital of France,Paris"], ["Write two success criteria.", "Two testable statements."]],
  x: ["Which subroutine will you write and test first?", "A named subroutine, such as load_questions."] },
{ t: "Assessed build", o: "I can build and test a program that uses lists, subroutines and files.",
  k: ["independent: working without help", "comment: a note in code starting with #", "test evidence: a record of tests with expected and actual results"],
  d: ["What should you do after writing each subroutine?", "Test it on its own before using it."],
  s: ["The task|Build your planned quiz on your own|Notes, your bug diary and your library are allowed|Save often",
      "Order|Load the questions and print them: test|Ask one question and return True or False: test|Loop, score, then save",
      "Hand in|Code with a comment for every subroutine|Completed test plan with actual results|One written trace of a function call"],
  a: "Build and test your quiz, complete the test plan and trace one function call by hand. Support: use the syntax help card and the load function provided. Stretch: add a random order and a top-five high-score table.",
  w: [["Name the subroutines in your program.", "A list."], ["Which line reads the question file?", "A line number and the code."], ["Record one test: input, expected, actual.", "A complete test."], ["Trace one call to your answer-checking function.", "A correct trace."], ["Which success criterion is not fully met?", "An honest evaluation."]],
  x: ["How many of your planned tests passed?", "A number."] },
{ t: "Feedback, code review and reflection", o: "I can review code, act on feedback and reflect on my learning.",
  k: ["code review: reading code to find problems and suggest improvements", "readability: how easy code is for another person to understand", "maintainable: easy to change and fix later"],
